## 2026-05-21T21:12:45Z - commit 03 / check enforce typed kernel crate dag

- Summary: Added `nixfied/project/check-kernel-crate-dag.sh`, a `cargo metadata --no-deps` checker that requires the initial typed kernel crates and rejects any local path dependency from `crates/kernel/*` to non-kernel workspace paths. Wired the checker into `nix run .#check` and the basic CI workflow as `task.ci.kernel-crate-dag`, and added a typed-kernel contract summary artifact containing `kernel_crates_present`.
- Tests run and results: `bash nixfied/project/check-kernel-crate-dag.sh --root . --self-test --summary-file /tmp/mfm-kernel-contract-summary.json` passed; the generated summary has `payload.kernel_crates_present=true` and `payload.crate_dag_passed=true`. Validator independently ran the same checker with a review summary path and `git diff --cached --check`; both passed. `nix run .#check` passed, including discovery verification, crate-DAG checks, formatting, and clippy.
- Design decisions: Enforced the kernel boundary as "kernel crates may only depend on local path crates under `crates/kernel/*`; registry dependencies remain allowed." This covers the explicitly denied old machine/sdk/bin/domain/ops/states paths and future local leakage without maintaining a fragile allowlist. The CI evidence is a project-owned typed-kernel contract artifact instead of changing the framework-owned workflow summary schema in this section.
- Doubts, risks, blockers, or review items: Validator reported no P0/P1 findings. Residual P2 risks: denied dependency self-tests inject each forbidden dependency into one kernel crate rather than every kernel crate, and `kernel_crates_present` is enforced in the project-owned typed-kernel contract artifact rather than merged into the framework-owned workflow summary schema. The contract artifact currently contains the first key required by the later `typed-kernel-contract` gate; later sections should extend the same artifact/schema as additional required keys land.

## 2026-05-21T21:38:42Z - commit 04 / kernel ids add strong identity types

- Summary: Replaced the skeletal `mfm-ids` crate with category-branded typed identities, digest algorithm and digest-byte parsing, checked constructors, kind/version-separated aliases, and golden tests for accepted and rejected identity strings. Added the new public typed-kernel crates to the docs publish catalog, docs wave, and umbrella README after the full test gate exposed the missing catalog entries.
- Tests run and results: `cargo fmt -p mfm-ids -- --check` passed after formatting; `cargo test -p mfm-ids` passed with 5 unit tests plus 2 doctests, including a compile-fail cross-kind assignment; `cargo clippy -p mfm-ids --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed. `cargo nextest run --cargo-profile ci -p mfm-publish-docs --test cli_integration yank_disallowed_by_catalog_renders_json_error_envelope` passed after catalog updates; `cargo test -p mfm-publish-docs catalog::tests --lib` passed; `nix run .#test` passed. Validator independently ran `git diff --cached --check`, crate fmt, crate tests, and crate clippy; all passed.
- Design decisions: Kept identity parsing dependency-free and limited the initial digest algorithm to `sha256-jcs-v1` per the RFC. Used private fields and branded type aliases so APIs can distinguish `SchemaId`, `SemanticTypeId`, `StateKind`, `ContentDigest`, and runtime ids at compile time. Kept `StateKind`/`CapabilityKind`/`AdapterKind`/`OperationKind` identities version-free and added separate typed `*Version` aliases. Treated the typed-kernel crates as public docs-rs packages because the skeleton commit added them as publishable workspace crates and the docs catalog gate requires every public workspace package to be explicitly cataloged.
- Doubts, risks, blockers, or review items: Validator reported no P0/P1 findings. Residual P2 risks: public checked constructors validate grammar and branding but not descriptor digest consistency yet, and identities currently store owned strings rather than supporting generated const identities. Digest consistency and canonical payload hashing are intentionally deferred to Commit 05 and later certification sections.

## 2026-05-21T21:54:31Z - commit 05 / kernel canonical add canonical json contract

- Summary: Implemented `mfm-canonical` with typed `CanonicalJsonBytes`, `CanonicalValue`, `CanonicalBytes`, `DecimalString`, `PlainCanonicalJsonBytes`, SHA-256 digest helpers, duplicate-key rejecting canonical object ordering, JCS-style UTF-16 key sorting, no-float/unsupported-number rejection, base64url-without-padding byte grammar, and variable/fixed decimal grammar checks.
- Tests run and results: `cargo fmt -p mfm-canonical -- --check` passed; `cargo test -p mfm-canonical` passed with 9 unit tests and 2 doctests, including a compile-fail guard that raw JSON is not a typed value canonicalizer; `cargo clippy -p mfm-canonical --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed; `git diff --check` passed; `nix run .#test` passed. Validator ran crate fmt, crate tests, crate clippy, `git diff --check`, and `cargo tree -p mfm-canonical --depth 1`; all passed.
- Design decisions: Split typed semantic canonicalization from plain JSON canonicalization. `CanonicalJsonBytes` is built from `CanonicalValue` so bytes and decimals must pass typed constructors before hashing. `PlainCanonicalJsonBytes` remains available for framework-owned already-lowered payloads such as future certified specs, where plain JSON strings are just strings and typed/schema semantics are established elsewhere. Added `ring` for SHA-256 using the existing workspace crypto dependency and kept dependencies limited to `mfm-ids`, `ring`, `serde`, and `serde_json`.
- Doubts, risks, blockers, or review items: Initial validator pass found a P1 that raw `CanonicalJsonBytes::from_json_str` could let decimal-looking or byte-looking strings bypass typed wrappers. The P1 was fixed by removing raw JSON constructors from `CanonicalJsonBytes`, removing public raw serde deserialization for `CanonicalValue`, and adding the separate documented `PlainCanonicalJsonBytes` boundary plus regression tests. Re-review reported no P0/P1 findings. Residual P2 risk: later spec/certification work must ensure the public plain-JSON path is not used as a semantic persisted-value bypass.

## 2026-05-21T22:20:05Z - commit 06 / kernel values add schema descriptors and value traits

- Summary: Implemented `mfm-values` with checked `MfmValue`, `MfmConfig`, and `PublicOutputDescriptor` traits; schema descriptors split into hash-defining `SchemaIdentity` and audit-only `SchemaAudit`; schema id derivation from canonical identity bytes; framework-owned `MaybeValue<T>` and `ArtifactRef<T>` constructors; `SkipReason`/`SkipCode`; strict no-secret/no-float persisted-surface policy types; and descriptor golden tests.
- Tests run and results: `cargo fmt -p mfm-values -- --check` passed; `cargo test -p mfm-values` passed with 8 unit tests and 1 doctest; `cargo clippy -p mfm-values --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed; `cargo test -p mfm-ids` passed; `cargo clippy -p mfm-ids --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed; `git diff --check` passed; `nix run .#test` passed. Validator ran the same narrow ids/values checks plus kernel grep for old dynamic APIs and found none.
- Design decisions: Kept schema descriptor construction checked and fallible rather than panicking or trusting convention. `SchemaIdentity::new` validates schema-name grammar and schema-kind/semantic-id invariants, and schema id derivation returns `Result<SchemaId>`. Removed the public unchecked static identity constructor from `mfm-ids`; framework generic identities are built through checked constructors. Kept `SchemaAudit` construction crate-private in this section so external crates cannot forge framework or derive provenance before the controlled derive path lands.
- Doubts, risks, blockers, or review items: Initial validator pass reported P1 findings for unchecked schema identity/schema-id panics and public unchecked identity/audit forgery. Both were addressed, and re-review reported no P0/P1 findings. Residual P2 risks: public `SchemaShape` enum variants can still bypass helper validation until derives/certification become authority, and Commit 07 must add a controlled derive/framework path for constructing derive provenance.

## 2026-05-22T00:24:18Z - commit 07 / kernel derive add value config output derives

- Summary: Implemented `mfm-program-derive` derives for `MfmValue`, `MfmConfig`, `StateInput`, `OperationOutput`, and `PublicOutputs`; added `StateInput`, `OperationOutput`, and `PublicOutputs` descriptor contracts to `mfm-values`; added derive-backed descriptor golden tests; added trybuild pass/fail fixtures covering downstream derives, unsupported serde attributes, floats, `serde_json::Value`, `HashMap`, skipped fields, custom serializers/defaults, known secret wrappers, and manual impl attempts. Also implemented the planned manual persisted-trait source-boundary checker and wired it into `nix run .#check` / CI summary output with `manual_value_config_output_impls_rejected`.
- Tests run and results: `cargo test -p mfm-program-derive` passed; `cargo clippy -p mfm-program-derive --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed; `cargo test -p mfm-values` passed; `cargo clippy -p mfm-values --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed; `nixfied/project/check-manual-persisted-impls.sh --root . --self-test --summary-file /tmp/mfm-manual-impl-summary.json` passed and produced `manual_value_config_output_impls_rejected=true`; `git diff --check` passed; `nix run .#check` passed; `nix run .#test` passed.
- Design decisions: Kept derives limited to named, non-generic structs in v1. Derives generate schema descriptors only and no scheduling/runtime logic. `serde(rename)` and supported `rename_all` forms resolve to concrete wire names; unsupported serde controls fail compile-time. `serde(default)` emits an `MfmDefault` bound instead of trusting `Default`. Floats, `usize`/`isize`, `HashMap`, raw `serde_json::Value`, and known secret wrappers including `Zeroizing` are rejected before descriptor generation. `MfmValue` semantic ids are derived from checked semantic identity fields at macro expansion time. Trybuild output is directed to a temp target dir so generated Cargo manifests do not pollute discovery artifacts. The manual-impl checker is parser-based rather than regex-based and rejects authored persisted trait impls and `__derive_generated` provenance calls outside framework allowlists, including alias and multiline forms.
- Doubts, risks, blockers, or review items: First validator pass found P1 issues that derive provenance was forgeable through the public hidden constructor and manual impl fixtures only covered one trait. Added source-boundary enforcement plus full manual-impl fixtures. Second validator pass found P1 bypasses in the regex checker through aliases and multiline impls. Replaced it with a Rust tokenizer/parser checker and added self-tests for aliased multiline manual impls and aliased forged provenance. Final re-review reported no remaining P0/P1 findings. Residual P2/P3 risks: source-boundary enforcement does not inspect generated code from build scripts/proc macros, alias tracking is repository-global and can conservatively false-positive future aliases, and self-tests cover alias/multiline behavior with one persisted-trait exemplar while the shared trait list applies the logic to all six traits.

## 2026-05-22T00:36:19Z - commit 09 / kernel effects add sealed effect markers

- Summary: Implemented `mfm-effects` sealed framework-owned effect markers for `Pure`, `ReadExternal`, `ManagedPlatformWrite`, and `ApplySideEffect`; added `EffectSpec`, `EffectClass`, `EffectDescriptor`, checked descriptor helpers, stable effect kind ids, and effect descriptor versions. Added typed `EffectKind` and `EffectVersion` identities in `mfm-ids` and golden tests for effect descriptor ids and class strings.
- Tests run and results: `cargo test -p mfm-effects` passed with unit tests and the compile-fail sealing doctest; `cargo clippy -p mfm-effects --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed; `cargo test -p mfm-ids` passed; `cargo clippy -p mfm-ids --all-targets -- -D warnings` passed; `nix run .#test` passed.
- Design decisions: Kept effect markers as uninhabited framework marker enums and sealed `EffectSpec` with a private trait so downstream states can select framework effects but cannot define new effect classes. Used checked identity/version constructors rather than const associated ids because the current branded identity types intentionally own validated strings. Derived effect kind digests from stable framework-owned effect names and pinned them with golden tests.
- Doubts, risks, blockers, or review items: Validator reported no P0/P1 findings and no old dynamic API leakage. Residual P2/P3 notes: future certification/spec code must treat descriptors derived from `S::Effect: EffectSpec` as authoritative rather than trusting arbitrary caller-constructed `EffectDescriptor` values, and the downstream sealing check is currently a compile-fail doctest rather than a trybuild fixture.
